# TypeScript/JavaScript (Node.js) security

Node.js-specific risks beyond the OWASP baseline in the common rules --
process/OS-level surface (child processes, the filesystem, the module
loader) that a Node runtime exposes and a generic web-security checklist
does not name directly.

## Command and process execution

- Never build a shell command string from untrusted input and pass it to
  `child_process.exec`/`execSync` -- the shell reinterprets `;`, `|`, `` ` ``,
  `$()` in the input. Use `execFile`/`execFileSync`/`spawn` with the
  command and an **argument array** (`spawn("git", ["log", userBranch])`),
  never string-concatenated into one command line.
- If `shell: true` is passed to `spawn`/`exec` at all, treat every argument
  as attacker-controlled and reject/escape it -- prefer removing `shell:
  true` over trying to escape correctly.

## Filesystem

- Resolve a user-supplied path with `path.resolve`/`path.join` against a
  fixed root, then verify the result still starts with that root
  (`resolved.startsWith(root + path.sep)`) before any `fs` call --
  otherwise `../../etc/passwd`-style traversal escapes the intended
  directory.
- Never pass an unvalidated path straight from a request/CLI arg to
  `fs.readFile`/`fs.writeFile`/`fs.unlink` without that containment check.

## Data handling

- Guard against prototype pollution when merging/assigning JSON into an
  existing object (`Object.assign`, a hand-rolled deep merge, or an older
  `lodash.merge`): reject or strip `__proto__`, `constructor`, and
  `prototype` keys, or use `Object.create(null)`/`structuredClone` for the
  target instead of a plain object literal.
- Never pass untrusted input to `eval`, `new Function(...)`, or a `vm`
  module context expecting it to sandbox safely -- Node's `vm` module is
  not a security boundary against a determined attacker; use a real
  out-of-process sandbox if untrusted code must run at all.
- Treat any `RegExp` built from user input, or a hand-written pattern with
  nested quantifiers (`(a+)+`, `(a|a)*`), as a ReDoS risk on
  attacker-controlled input length -- prefer a linear-time match, an
  input length cap before the regex runs, or a known-safe regex engine.

## Network and secrets

- Validate and allowlist the target of any outbound `fetch`/`http(s)`
  request built from user input (a URL, hostname, or webhook target) --
  an unchecked outbound request lets an attacker probe internal services
  (SSRF), including cloud metadata endpoints (`169.254.169.254`).
- Never `console.log`/log a request body, header, or env var wholesale in
  a path that might carry a token, password, or API key -- log an
  identifier (user id, request id), not the credential-bearing payload.
- Run `npm audit` (or the project's package manager's equivalent) and keep
  the lockfile committed and in sync with `package.json`; do not add a
  dependency with a known-critical advisory without a documented reason.

## Runtime posture

- Where the deployment target supports it, know that Node's permission
  model (`--permission` plus `--allow-fs-read`/`--allow-fs-write`/
  `--allow-child-process`) exists to restrict what a process may touch --
  flag when a service handling untrusted input runs with no such
  restriction and no containerization either.
